    "result_text": "All 30 tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS), including the new `Cp19Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the daily owner-creation limit in the Spring PetClinic REST app, following the existing `CityAtCapacityException` pattern used for the per-city capacity rule.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New exception** \u2014 `rest/advice/DailyOwnerLimitException.java`: a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending `LocalDate`, documented as mapping to 429.\n\n2. **Exception handler** \u2014 `rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java`: added `handleDailyOwnerLimitException` returning `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429) with a `ProblemDetail` body, matching the structure of the sibling handlers.\n\n3. **Controller check** \u2014 `rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n   - Added the `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT = 100` constant.\n   - Added `requireDailyLimitNotReached()`, which counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals today's date (`LocalDate.now()`) and throws when the count is \u2265 100.\n   - Called it in `addOwner(...)` before persisting (after the other quota/duplicate checks).\n\nThe rule rejects creating an owner once 100 or more owners already have today's `registrationDate`, responding with 429 \u2014 exactly what `Cp19Tests.coreRejectsOverDailyLimit` (create 100, then the 101st is rejected) asserts. The full suite (30 tests) is green.",
